        "AR94-NC2: could not observe a genuine OS-created sandbox with its creator confirmably alive across \
         {MAX_ATTEMPTS} attempts -- either sandbox creation is not reliably observable in this environment, or \
         the creator's own run is too fast relative to the probe's own subprocess-spawn cost"
    );
}

#[test]
fn ar94_nc2_a_genuine_os_created_sandbox_still_receives_the_exemption() {
    let (root, _g) = fresh("ar94nc2");
    let machine = root.clone();

    // A: cross-process, while the creator is confirmably still running. **Measured, not assumed (second
    // iteration of this test):** an earlier version probed with a SEPARATE `gov` subprocess here. Even with the
    // `sandbox_is_ready` fix above, that still raced `scheduler/mod.rs`'s own sandbox lifecycle under the
    // required full suite's own concurrency: that sandbox is dropped the moment its OWN family's checks finish,
    // in-process, synchronously -- nothing in the product ever reads it from a SEPARATE subprocess, so there is
    // no natural lower bound on how long it survives, and spawning a whole second `gov` binary (fork+exec, then
    // its own full command run) is slow enough, under load, to lose that race. This version removes the
    // subprocess-spawn delay from the probe entirely: `gov_runtime::paths::adoption_floor_anchor_path_at` is the
    // exact function `FloorIdentity::path`/`reconcile` call internally to decide the exemption (`None` iff
    // `is_health_sandbox_root`) -- calling it directly, in-process, from THIS test binary (a process distinct
    // from the creator either way, so still a genuine cross-process read) proves the identical product decision
    // with no added latency. `gov_runtime` is already linked (every other helper in this file calls into it).
    let machine_a = machine.clone();
    let (genuine, a_exempt) = spawn_and_probe_while_creator_alive(
        &root,
        &machine,
        "S-ar94nc2-genuine",
        &["verify", "governance"],
        move |p| gov_runtime::paths::adoption_floor_anchor_path_at(&machine_state_dir(&machine_a), p).is_none(),
    );
    eprintln!(
        "AR94-NC2 A (cross-process, creator confirmed alive before & after) genuine={} exempt={}",
        genuine.display(),
        a_exempt
    );
    assert!(
        a_exempt,
        "AR94-NC2 (A): a GENUINE OS-created sandbox must still receive the bootstrap exemption cross-process \
         while its creator is alive -- the repair must not have closed the hole by refusing everything \
         (adoption_floor_anchor_path_at returned Some, meaning is_health_sandbox_root read false)"
    );
    // `genuine` (case A) is NOT reused below: `scheduler/mod.rs`'s per-family sandbox is dropped the moment that
    // ONE family's own checks finish, which can race ahead of this test even though the CREATOR PROCESS (still
    // working through later families) remains alive -- confirmed by observation (an initial version of this test
    // that reused it here hit `NotFound` copying it). B2 below gets its own, freshly-killed (hence frozen, stable
    // to copy from) sandbox instead, using the SAME proven kill-on-ready technique `ar94_c1`/`ar94_d0` already
    // use, rather than racing natural cleanup.
    let _ = genuine;

    // B2: recorded, but its creator has since exited (killed the instant a populated, non-skill sandbox is
    // observed -- the same technique `spawn_and_kill_while_sandboxed` uses elsewhere in this file, so this
    // sandbox is FROZEN, never mid-natural-cleanup, by the time it is examined). P2-AR-0096's own defining
    // negative case: nothing about the directory itself is ever touched.
    let sbdir = root.join(".governance-runtime").join("health").join("sandboxes");
    let ready_dir = sbdir.clone();
    let (live, polls) = spawn_and_kill_when(&root, &machine, "S-ar94nc2-dead", &["verify", "governance"], move |_| {
        ls(&ready_dir)
            .iter()
            .any(|n| !n.starts_with("skill-") && sandbox_is_ready(&ready_dir.join(n)))
    });
